.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/yocto-queue Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "yocto-queue [![](https://badgen.net/bundlephobia/minzip/yocto-queue)](https://bundlephobia.com/result?p=yocto-queue)"
.PP
.RS 4
Tiny queue data structure 
.RE
.PP

.PP
You should use this package instead of an array if you do a lot of \fRArray#push()\fP and \fRArray#shift()\fP on large arrays, since \fRArray#shift()\fP has \fRlinear time complexity\fP \fIO(n)\fP while \fRQueue#dequeue()\fP has \fRconstant time complexity\fP \fIO(1)\fP\&. That makes a huge difference for large arrays\&.

.PP
.RS 4
A \fRqueue\fP is an ordered list of elements where an element is inserted at the end of the queue and is removed from the front of the queue\&. A queue works based on the first-in, first-out (\fRFIFO\fP) principle\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install yocto\-queue
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
const Queue = require('yocto\-queue');

const queue = new Queue();

queue\&.enqueue('🦄');
queue\&.enqueue('🌈');

console\&.log(queue\&.size);
//=> 2

console\&.log(\&.\&.\&.queue);
//=> '🦄 🌈'

console\&.log(queue\&.dequeue());
//=> '🦄'

console\&.log(queue\&.dequeue());
//=> '🌈'
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "\fRqueue = new Queue()\fP"
The instance is an \fR\fRIterable\fP\fP, which means you can iterate over the queue front to back with a “for…of” loop, or use spreading to convert the queue to an array\&. Don't do this unless you really need to though, since it's slow\&.
.SS "\fR\&.enqueue(value)\fP"
Add a value to the queue\&.
.SS "\fR\&.dequeue()\fP"
Remove the next value in the queue\&.

.PP
Returns the removed value or \fRundefined\fP if the queue is empty\&.
.SS "\fR\&.clear()\fP"
Clear the queue\&.
.SS "\fR\&.size\fP"
The size of the queue\&.
